  "notes": "The Hitmen's multi-instrumentalist, and the research explains his harmony by his method: \"genuine ii–V–I and secondary-dominant motion **because he arranges and replays rather than loops**\". So this is the only entry on this axis carrying a raised `borrowed.V` — 0.35 against the engine default's 0.1 — which is how a style model says secondary dominant without a chord-symbol vocabulary for one. The rest follows: ii–V–I leads the families, ninths run at 0.2, voicings are open across a 30-semitone register, and the bass is `independent_riff` at 0.45 with \"octave leaps and syncopation\" written into its passing tones and 0.42 anticipation. Live percussion and guitar overdubs give four perc lanes and a looser 0.84 quantize than the programmed Bad Boy cuts. \"Sung choruses with harmony stacks\" is `countermelody.densityRatio` 0.6 with `fillGapsOnly` off. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high: the role and method are first-person, no drum or tempo numbers are. The specific inferred parameter is `chords.borrowed.V` at 0.35 — the research says secondary-dominant motion happens, not how often. ⚠ The vi–ii–V–I family is INFERRED; the research names ii–V–I and \"4–8 bar progressions\" without listing the rest. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 49 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 50 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1. ⚠ `countermelody.fillGapsOnly` returned to true. With it off and a density of 0.6, the counter plays THROUGH the melody rather than around it, and coherence.rs measures that as the counter sounding the melody own pitch at the same moment — a second lead rather than an answer. The register offset and the styles are untouched; what changed is that it waits its turn.",
